## Migration: `Thread.Abort` Timeout → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code forcibly aborts a thread after a timeout. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` is gone (`SYSLIB0006`).

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Preferred: async overload
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await work(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---------|------------|
| **Work doesn't actually stop** | The `Func<T>` overload only abandons the task — it keeps running in the background. To truly stop work, use the async overload and pass the `CancellationToken` into your work delegate. |
| **Not checking the token inside work** | Insert `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at checkpoints in loops or between expensive steps. |
| **Leaking `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always wrap in `using`. Leaking it causes timer leaks. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)` to distinguish timeout from external cancellation. |
| **Blocking sync I/O ignores the token** | `Thread.Sleep`, `socket.Read`, etc. won't respond to cancellation. Use async I/O or `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne()`. |

### What Changed Fundamentally

**Old model (preemptive):** The runtime injects `ThreadAbortException` into the target thread at any point — work stops involuntarily.

**New model (cooperative):** The work must voluntarily check `CancellationToken` and exit. If the `Func<T>` delegate never checks the token, it runs to completion even after timeout — you've only abandoned *waiting* for it.

### If Work Cannot Be Modified (Uncooperative Code)

Move it to a child process and use `Process.Kill()`:

```csharp
// Last resort for truly uncooperative code
var process = Process.Start("worker.exe", args);
if (!process.WaitForExit(timeout))
{
    process.Kill(entireProcessTree: true);
    throw new TimeoutException("...");
}
```

This is the only way to forcibly stop uncooperative managed code in modern .NET.
